.TH BRICK 1
.SH NAME
brick \- pair with, configure and mount a styxbrick
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B brick key
[
.B -d
.I keydir
]
.br
.B brick pair
[
.B -d
.I keydir
]
.I usbmnt
.br
.B brick wifi
.I usbmnt
.br
.B brick mount
[
.B -abu
] [
.B -d
.I keydir
]
.I addr
.I mountpoint
.br
.B brick d2
[
.B -d
.I keydir
] [
.B -l
.I leasems
]
.I brickmnt
.I dir
.SH DESCRIPTION
A styxbrick is a microcontroller that serves its hardware
(LED, GPIO pins, ADC, temperature sensor) as a Styx file tree.
On its USB serial port it serves the tree without authentication,
and also a
.B /config
directory; physical access is the trust boundary there.
That port is mounted with
.IR mount (1)'s
.B -A
option, for example
.EX
	mount -A '#U*/dev/ttyACM0' /n/brick
.EE
(after setting the host's tty to raw mode).
On the network the brick serves only to the client it was paired with:
inside TLS 1.3 on TCP, or over Noise-secured datagrams on UDP
(see
.IR sdg (2)),
which a lossy or intermittent link favours.
Each side identifies the other by a pin: the SHA-256 hash
of its certificate's SubjectPublicKeyInfo, in hex.
No certificate authority is involved.
.PP
.B "brick key"
prints our pin, making a key and certificate first if there are none.
.PP
.B "brick pair"
gives the brick mounted over USB at
.I usbmnt
our pin (in
.IB usbmnt /config/peer\fR)
and saves its pin (read from
.IB usbmnt /config/spki\fR)
as
.IB keydir /brick.spki\fR.
If the brick serves datagrams
.RB ( /config/sdg ),
it also gives the brick our X25519 public key and a fresh pre-shared key,
and saves the brick's public key;
and if the brick has safety outputs
.RB ( /config/d2 ),
a fresh D2 safety key.
Because all of it travels over the USB cable, nothing the network
could alter is trusted.
.PP
.B "brick wifi"
copies standard input, which should be
.EX
	ssid \fIname\fP
	pass \fIpassphrase\fP
.EE
to
.IB usbmnt /config/wifi\fR
in a single write.
The brick never lets the passphrase be read back.
.PP
.B "brick mount"
dials
.I addr
(port 564 if none is given),
makes a TLS 1.3 connection presenting our certificate and accepting
only the server key pinned in
.IB keydir /brick.spki\fR,
and mounts the brick's tree on
.IR mountpoint .
The
.B -a
and
.B -b
options are as for
.IR bind (1).
The connection is closed when the tree is unmounted.
With
.BR -u ,
it mounts over datagrams instead
(UDP port 5640 if none is given; the address may be a dial string such as
.BR udp!host!5640 ).
Then every request gets an answer: the brick's reply, or an error made
locally when its deadline (5 s) passes
.RB ( "timed out" ;
for a write,
.BR "outcome unknown: timed out" ),
or when a read the brick has acknowledged as waiting for data stops being
acknowledged
.RB ( "link down" ).
Nothing blocks on a lost link, the session outlives outages, and a read
that waits for data (such as
.BR /events )
may wait as long as it needs.
.PP
.B "brick d2"
holds the brick's safety outputs live on behalf of applications.
A safety output is driven only by D2 commands: each carries a fresh grant
from the brick, a lease, a sequence number and a MAC under the safety key,
and the brick takes the output to its configured failsafe value when the
lease runs out.
.B "brick d2"
opens a D2 session on the brick mounted at
.I brickmnt
and serves, in
.IR dir ,
a file
.B ctl
and a file for each safety output, named for it
.RB ( pwm.2 ,
.BR gpio.5 ).
Writing a value (a pulse width in microseconds, or a level) to an output's
file holds the output at that value: it is renewed under fresh grants every
.IR leasems /5
(default lease 500 ms).
Writing
.B release
ends its use: it goes to failsafe at once, and the others are untouched.
Reading it gives its value and state.
Writing
.B arm
to
.B ctl
arms the session, which values need;
.B safe
takes every output to failsafe.
Arming is never automatic.
If the link fails or this program dies, renewals stop and the brick takes
every output to failsafe within a lease, then refuses commands until an
application writes
.B arm
again; reading
.B ctl
says why.
.PP
.I Keydir
defaults to
.BI /usr/ user /keyring/brick
and holds
.TP
.B client.key
our P-256 private key, as 64 hex digits (mode 600)
.TP
.B client.crt
our self-signed X.509 certificate, in DER
.TP
.B brick.spki
the brick's pin
.TP
.B sdg.key
our X25519 private key, hex (mode 600)
.TP
.B sdg.psk
the pre-shared key given to the brick, hex (mode 600)
.TP
.B sdg.brick
the brick's X25519 public key
.TP
.B sdg.ctr
the datagram handshake counter, which never repeats or goes back
.TP
.B d2.key
the D2 safety key given to the brick, hex (mode 600)
.SH EXAMPLE
Pair over USB, give the brick its network, then use it from anywhere:
.EX
	mount -A '#U*/dev/ttyACM0' /n/brick
	brick pair /n/brick
	brick wifi /n/brick <creds
	cat /n/brick/net/status
	unmount /n/brick
	brick mount -u 192.168.1.23 /n/brick
	echo 1 >/n/brick/led
.EE
Hold a servo on a safety output, then let it go:
.EX
	brick d2 /n/brick /n/d2
	echo arm >/n/d2/ctl
	echo 1600 >/n/d2/pwm.2
	echo release >/n/d2/pwm.2
.EE
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/brick.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR mount (1),
.IR bind (1),
.IR sdg (2)
